# Documentation Standards

Documentation has **two layers**. Both are required where they apply. They answer different questions and **do not replace each other**.

| Layer | Question it answers | Where |
|-------|---------------------|--------|
| **Public contract** | What does this API do? What goes in and out? | `///` / TSDoc on public types, methods, and properties |
| **Algorithm** | How does this implementation work step by step? | `//` comments before each logical block inside method bodies |

When the user asks to document code, they mean **both** unless they specify otherwise.

## Public Contract (Required on Public API)

**C#**

- `/// <summary>` on public types, public methods, and public properties
- `/// <param name="...">` for each method parameter
- `/// <returns>` when the method returns a value
- Keep summaries concise and action-oriented

**TypeScript**

- TSDoc on public types, classes, and public methods
- `@param` and `@returns` where applicable

**Type-level summaries** may use a sentence or two for scope and boundaries — not in Cursor rules or `<remarks>`.

## Algorithm Comments (Required in Method Bodies)

- Add a brief `//` comment on its own line **before each logical block** in a method body
- Cover every distinct step: validation, branching, I/O, computation, error handling, transaction boundaries
- Write action-oriented sentences starting with verbs ("Resolve the command type", "Roll back the transaction", "Skip types that are not concrete classes")
- Apply to **public and private methods alike** when the body has non-trivial logic
- **Never inline** — comments always sit on their own line above the code they describe
- Skip body comments only for truly trivial one-liners (e.g. `return userDao.GetByIdAsync(id, cancellationToken);`)

## What Good Looks Like

Public contract **and** algorithm comments together:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Resolve the command CLR type from the envelope command name.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="commandTypeName">Assembly-qualified or full type name from the envelope.</param>
/// <returns>The command type, or null when the name cannot be resolved.</returns>
private static Type? ResolveCommandType(string commandTypeName)
{
    // Try assembly-qualified name first (what EnqueueAsync writes today).
    var commandType = Type.GetType(commandTypeName, throwOnError: false);

    if (commandType != null)
    {
        return commandType;
    }

    // Fall back to full name in the domain assembly (older queued messages).
    return typeof(AssemblyMarker).Assembly.GetType(commandTypeName);
}
```

## What Bad Looks Like

- Public method with `/// <summary>` but **no** body comments explaining the steps
- Method body full of `//` comments but **no** contract docs on a **public** method
- Replacing one layer with the other when both apply
